<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Plataea</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Plataea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Boeotian city that defied Thebes, stood with Athens at Marathon and gave its name to the battle that ended the Persian invasion of Greece in 479 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> lay on the northern slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cithaeron">Mount Cithaeron</a>, on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>. When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> pressed it to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League</a> in the late 6th century BCE, the Plataeans turned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, traditionally in 519 BCE. That alliance shaped the rest of their history: alone among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, they sent their whole levy to fight beside the Athenians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Marathon</a> in 490 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A modest farming community governed by its own citizen assembly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> mattered far beyond its size. In 479 BCE the allied Greek army under the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> defeated the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> on its territory, and the city was charged with tending the graves of the fallen and hosting a festival of freedom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleutheria-plataea">Eleutheria</a>. Its people spoke the Boeotian dialect and kept close cultural ties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Theban aggression began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> at Plataea in 431 BCE; after a famous siege the city surrendered to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> and was razed in 427 BCE. Restored in 386 BCE, it was destroyed again by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> in 373 BCE and rebuilt under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> and Alexander. It then belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League</a> until Roman conquest in 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/plataea.jpg" alt="Plataea" width="320" height="181"><figcaption>Image: Geraki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plataea_battlefield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plataea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Boeotian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Plataia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-373&amp;focus=plataea" aria-label="Plataea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 805 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Plataea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5579 1061L5562 1098M5579 1061L5569 1054"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5569 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5579 1061h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5579 1061h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-373&amp;focus=plataea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

